Size the enclave so it can actually be created on a real machine.

Upstream ships NumHeapPages=36700160, i.e. a **140 GB** enclave heap.  On a
no-EDMM kernel the whole heap is committed at enclave creation, so this either
exceeds physical memory outright or spends many minutes paging before the first
query runs.  It is a placeholder value, not a measured requirement: every TM
query evaluated here fits in 4 GB.

  NumHeapPages  36700160 -> 1048576   (140 GB -> 4 GB)
  NumStackPages   262144 ->   65536   (1 GB -> 256 MB)

4 GB is the same heap the single-ecall engine is given in every comparison
(heap_4g.xml), so neither engine is handed an advantage in enclave-creation
cost -- which is reported separately as Init, and is dominated by heap size.

baselines/obliviator/run_tests.py also rewrites NumHeapPages and re-signs before
each run, so a harness-driven campaign overrides this.  The patch matters for
the state the build script leaves behind: without it, a freshly built enclave
cannot be loaded at all.

--- a/join/enclave/parallel.conf
+++ b/join/enclave/parallel.conf
@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@
 Debug=1
-NumHeapPages=36700160
-NumStackPages=262144
+NumHeapPages=1048576
+NumStackPages=65536
 NumTCS=32
 ProductID=1
 SecurityVersion=1
\ No newline at end of file
